WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.492
Here is a sorted array of student IDs

00:00:03.492 --> 00:00:04.584
five values

00:00:04.584 --> 00:00:07.524
in an int array with room for ten.

00:00:07.524 --> 00:00:10.632
We want to insert 1005

00:00:10.632 --> 00:00:12.900
and keep the array sorted.

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:15.848
The loop starts at the last element.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.941
2040 is bigger

00:00:18.013 --> 00:00:19.673
than 1005

00:00:19.673 --> 00:00:22.198
so it moves one cell to the right.

00:00:22.198 --> 00:00:23.786
So does 2000

00:00:23.786 --> 00:00:25.590
then 1050

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:27.250
then 1010.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.523
1000 is smaller, so the loop stops

00:00:30.591 --> 00:00:32.358
and 1005 goes

00:00:32.426 --> 00:00:33.717
into the free cell.

00:00:33.717 --> 00:00:36.640
Four elements moved, to make room for one.

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:39.607
Now delete 1010.

00:00:39.607 --> 00:00:42.675
First a loop finds it, in cell two.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:45.573
Then every element after it moves

00:00:45.658 --> 00:00:49.153
one cell to the left: 1050

00:00:49.153 --> 00:00:52.392
2000, and 2040.

00:00:52.392 --> 00:00:53.500
Three moves.

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:56.211
The count drops from six to five.

00:00:56.283 --> 00:00:58.286
Cell five still holds a copy

00:00:58.357 --> 00:00:59.931
of 2040

00:00:59.931 --> 00:01:02.650
but it is no longer part of the list.

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:05.543
And the program prints exactly that

00:01:05.543 --> 00:01:08.048
four elements moved for the insertion

00:01:08.048 --> 00:01:09.590
three for the deletion.

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:10.825
Now predict.

00:01:10.825 --> 00:01:13.187
In the same five-element array

00:01:13.261 --> 00:01:15.622
how many elements move to insert

00:01:15.696 --> 00:01:17.540
999?

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:19.754
And to insert 3000?

00:01:19.754 --> 00:01:21.820
Pause the video and decide.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:28.840
999 goes to the front

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:31.000
so all five elements move.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.560
3000 goes to the end

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:34.760
so none moves.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:37.320
Same array: best case O(1).

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:38.576
In general

00:01:38.658 --> 00:01:41.787
inserting or deleting at position i of

00:01:41.869 --> 00:01:44.751
n elements moves n - i of them

00:01:44.751 --> 00:01:47.798
none at the end, all n at the front

00:01:47.798 --> 00:01:50.021
and about half on average.

00:01:50.021 --> 00:01:53.150
So insertion and deletion are O(n).

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.408
And the size is fixed

00:01:55.495 --> 00:01:58.183
new int[100] reserves

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:00.524
exactly 100 cells

00:02:00.524 --> 00:02:04.340
so you must know an upper limit in advance.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:07.720
An array is one way to store a list.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:09.276
But what is a list?

00:02:09.353 --> 00:02:12.153
A list is an abstract data type that

00:02:12.231 --> 00:02:14.876
holds a finite sequence of values

00:02:14.876 --> 00:02:17.598
in a definite order: a1, a2,

00:02:17.598 --> 00:02:18.453
up to aN.

00:02:18.453 --> 00:02:21.720
The same value may appear more than once

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:23.120
like 12 here.

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:25.458
N is the size of the list

00:02:25.535 --> 00:02:27.676
and the list with N equal to

00:02:27.753 --> 00:02:29.512
0 is the empty list.

00:02:29.512 --> 00:02:31.653
a1 is the first element

00:02:31.653 --> 00:02:33.029
and aN the last.

00:02:33.106 --> 00:02:34.865
Each element except the

00:02:34.941 --> 00:02:37.924
last has a successor, the one after it

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.065
and each element except the

00:02:40.142 --> 00:02:43.430
first has a predecessor, the one before it.

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:45.940
Abstract means that the ADT

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:47.940
says what a list can do

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:49.700
not how it is stored.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:52.260
An array is one implementation.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:55.220
This lecture is about the other one

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:56.820
a linked structure.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:59.844
The operations of the List ADT.

00:02:59.844 --> 00:03:02.525
The names vary from book to book.

00:03:02.525 --> 00:03:05.758
printList prints the elements in order

00:03:05.758 --> 00:03:08.360
and makeEmpty removes them all.

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:11.352
find returns the position of the

00:03:11.434 --> 00:03:13.580
first occurrence of a key.

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:15.892
find(52) gives 3.

00:03:15.892 --> 00:03:18.616
find(12) gives 2, not 5

00:03:18.616 --> 00:03:21.670
because it stops at the first match.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:25.364
insert(99) at position 3 makes

00:03:25.444 --> 00:03:27.832
99 the third element

00:03:27.912 --> 00:03:30.301
and the elements after it each

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:31.973
move back one place.

00:03:31.973 --> 00:03:35.157
delete(52), on the original list

00:03:35.237 --> 00:03:37.228
removes the first element

00:03:37.307 --> 00:03:38.820
equal to 52.

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:41.539
Positions here count from 1

00:03:41.539 --> 00:03:42.960
as on the slides.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:46.434
Java's own List interface counts from 0

00:03:46.434 --> 00:03:48.250
so keep the two apart.

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:51.192
A linked list stores each element

00:03:51.264 --> 00:03:54.055
in its own small object, called a node

00:03:54.055 --> 00:03:56.632
together with a reference, or link

00:03:56.632 --> 00:03:57.921
to the next node.

00:03:57.921 --> 00:04:00.569
The nodes can be anywhere in memory

00:04:00.569 --> 00:04:03.432
only the links hold the order together.

00:04:03.432 --> 00:04:05.723
On paper we draw them in a row

00:04:05.723 --> 00:04:07.870
with one arrow for each link.

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:09.856
What does that give us?

00:04:09.856 --> 00:04:10.995
A dynamic size

00:04:11.066 --> 00:04:13.983
the list grows by one Node on each insert

00:04:14.054 --> 00:04:16.473
and shrinks by one on each delete

00:04:16.473 --> 00:04:19.176
with no upper limit fixed in advance.

00:04:19.176 --> 00:04:21.595
And cheap insertion and deletion

00:04:21.595 --> 00:04:23.018
nothing is shifted.

00:04:23.018 --> 00:04:25.365
Once you are at the right place

00:04:25.365 --> 00:04:27.500
only one or two links change.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:28.780
That is O(1).

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:31.204
And what does it cost?

00:04:31.204 --> 00:04:32.461
First, memory.

00:04:32.461 --> 00:04:36.066
An int array needs 4 bytes per element.

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:39.167
A Node holding one int also stores a

00:04:39.251 --> 00:04:42.185
reference and a Java object header

00:04:42.185 --> 00:04:44.280
about 24 bytes.

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:47.473
Second, there is no random access.

00:04:47.557 --> 00:04:50.985
To reach the i-th element you must follow

00:04:51.069 --> 00:04:53.828
i - 1 links from the front.

00:04:53.828 --> 00:04:56.336
That is O(i), not O(1)

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:58.761
so binary search on a linked

00:04:58.845 --> 00:05:00.350
list is pointless.

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:02.725
Third, there is no way back

00:05:02.797 --> 00:05:04.533
from a Node you can only

00:05:04.605 --> 00:05:06.052
reach its successor.

00:05:06.052 --> 00:05:08.438
A doubly linked list fixes that

00:05:08.438 --> 00:05:11.620
at the cost of one more reference per Node.

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:14.985
There are four kinds of linked list.

00:05:14.985 --> 00:05:16.919
In a singly linked list

00:05:16.919 --> 00:05:19.240
each Node has one link, next

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:21.793
and the last Node links to null.

00:05:21.793 --> 00:05:24.965
A doubly linked list adds a second link

00:05:24.965 --> 00:05:26.976
prev, to the Node before.

00:05:26.976 --> 00:05:28.523
In a circular list

00:05:28.523 --> 00:05:31.540
the last Node links back to the first

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:34.480
and a doubly circular list does both.

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:36.862
Parts 1 to 3 are about

00:05:36.934 --> 00:05:38.603
the singly linked list.

00:05:38.603 --> 00:05:41.070
Part 4 covers the other three.

00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:44.290
In Java, a Node is a small class.

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:47.810
It has an int field, element, for the data

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:50.370
and a field next, of type Node

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:53.170
a reference to the following Node.

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:55.250
The constructor stores x

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:57.090
and sets next to null.

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:00.761
To use a list, you only keep one reference

00:06:00.761 --> 00:06:03.001
the head, to the first Node.

00:06:03.079 --> 00:06:05.087
Everything else is reached

00:06:05.164 --> 00:06:07.018
by following next links.

00:06:07.018 --> 00:06:09.335
The last node's next is null

00:06:09.413 --> 00:06:11.575
and the empty list is simply

00:06:11.653 --> 00:06:13.120
head equal to null.

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:15.539
A warning about the word head.

00:06:15.539 --> 00:06:16.505
In some code

00:06:16.505 --> 00:06:18.988
head refers to the first real node.

00:06:18.988 --> 00:06:21.954
In other code, head is an extra dummy node

00:06:22.023 --> 00:06:23.609
that stores no element

00:06:23.609 --> 00:06:26.506
and the first real node is head.next.

00:06:26.575 --> 00:06:28.575
Always check which convention

00:06:28.644 --> 00:06:30.230
the code you read uses.

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:32.929
To traverse a list is to

00:06:33.017 --> 00:06:34.960
visit every node once.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:38.756
printList starts with curr equal to head.

00:06:38.756 --> 00:06:40.875
While curr is not null

00:06:40.875 --> 00:06:43.347
it prints curr.element.

00:06:43.347 --> 00:06:45.731
Here, that is 34.

00:06:45.731 --> 00:06:48.026
Then it follows the link

00:06:48.026 --> 00:06:50.410
curr equals curr.next.

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:54.806
12, 52, 16, 12.

00:06:54.806 --> 00:06:58.968
Now curr is null, so the loop ends

00:06:58.968 --> 00:07:01.280
and it prints null.

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:05.268
Counting and searching are traversals too.

00:07:05.268 --> 00:07:07.620
Size counts the nodes: 5.

00:07:07.702 --> 00:07:10.460
find walks with a position counter

00:07:10.541 --> 00:07:12.083
that starts at 1.

00:07:12.083 --> 00:07:14.517
find(52) returns 3.

00:07:14.517 --> 00:07:17.356
find(12) returns 2, not 5

00:07:17.356 --> 00:07:19.709
it stops at the first match.

00:07:19.709 --> 00:07:22.954
And find(99) walks off the end

00:07:22.954 --> 00:07:24.820
and returns -1.

00:07:25.170 --> 00:07:27.475
A traversal takes O(n) time.

00:07:27.475 --> 00:07:30.472
A search is O(1) in the best case

00:07:30.472 --> 00:07:33.161
when the key is in the first node

00:07:33.161 --> 00:07:35.466
and O(n) in the worst case

00:07:35.466 --> 00:07:38.770
when the key is last, or not there at all.

00:07:39.350 --> 00:07:42.198
A common slip is to write the loop test

00:07:42.271 --> 00:07:44.827
as curr.next not equal to null.

00:07:44.827 --> 00:07:47.603
Then the loop stops at the last node

00:07:47.603 --> 00:07:49.136
instead of after it

00:07:49.136 --> 00:07:52.350
so printList never prints the last 12.

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:54.979
And on an empty list, curr is null

00:07:55.052 --> 00:07:56.877
so curr.next throws a

00:07:56.950 --> 00:07:58.630
NullPointerException.

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:02.076
Stopping on the last node is the right test

00:08:02.148 --> 00:08:04.524
only when you want the last node

00:08:04.524 --> 00:08:06.900
as insertLast does in part two.

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:08.384
To sum up.

00:08:08.384 --> 00:08:12.271
One: a list is a finite, ordered sequence

00:08:12.271 --> 00:08:14.350
an abstract data type

00:08:14.350 --> 00:08:17.061
and positions count from 1.

00:08:17.061 --> 00:08:17.513
Two

00:08:17.513 --> 00:08:21.491
an array gives instant access to element i

00:08:21.491 --> 00:08:23.750
but it has a fixed size

00:08:23.841 --> 00:08:26.100
and inserting or deleting

00:08:26.191 --> 00:08:28.360
shifts up to n elements.

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:29.235
Three

00:08:29.235 --> 00:08:32.905
a singly linked list is a chain of nodes

00:08:32.905 --> 00:08:36.226
each with an element and a next link

00:08:36.226 --> 00:08:38.236
reached from the head.

00:08:38.323 --> 00:08:40.420
A traversal follows next

00:08:40.508 --> 00:08:42.430
until it reaches null.

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:43.517
Try this.

00:08:43.599 --> 00:08:46.139
Write a method that returns the

00:08:46.221 --> 00:08:48.105
last element of a list.

00:08:48.105 --> 00:08:50.808
Test it first on the empty list

00:08:50.808 --> 00:08:53.020
and on a list of one node.

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:56.641
In part two, we insert and delete nodes

00:08:56.641 --> 00:08:58.930
without breaking the chain.

